---
import { HeroSim } from "../components/HeroSim.tsx";
import HandbookCard from "../components/HandbookCard.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../components/SiteNav.astro";
import DocsState from "../components/DocsState.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../lib/urls";
import { HANDBOOKS } from "../data/handbooks";
import "../components/HeroSim.css";
import "../styles/docs.css";

/**
 * O que o motor faz, e por que ele existe.
 *
 * Aqui viviam os quatro níveis fixos, numerados e iguais para todo assunto.
 * Eles morreram quando profundidade virou a árvore de composição, e a seção
 * continuou vendendo a escada única. Ver `DECISIONS.md` §9: abrir uma CPU até o
 * fio é descida de verdade, e um trace não tem *dentro* — ele tem árvore
 * espalhada por processos que só se encontram no Collector.
 *
 * Os nomes velhos não são escritos aqui de propósito: a varredura de
 * `landing.spec.ts` procura por eles em `apps/site/src` para que a escada não
 * volte por descuido, e uma guarda que abre exceção para comentário deixa de
 * ser guarda.
 */
const GESTOS = [
  {
    name: "Open a part",
    line: "Double-click any box and you are inside it, looking at the parts it is made of.",
  },
  {
    name: "Open a message",
    line: "The thing moving on the wire has a body, and you can read it field by field.",
  },
  {
    name: "Follow one item",
    line: "Click it and its whole path opens up, with what each stop changed lit where it lives.",
  },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="OpenLabs"
  description="Handbooks that run: every subject modelled as a simulation you can take apart, on one engine that does not know what the subject is."
>
  <SiteNav current="home" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="hero editorial">
      <div>
        <h1>See how the thing actually works.</h1>
        <p class="hero__lede prose">
          OpenLabs builds handbooks that run. Most material shows you a diagram
          and a config file; here the diagram is the running mechanism — change
          something, watch it react, then open it up and read the exact data
          that produced what you just saw.
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          One engine, many subjects. Below is a piece of the OpenTelemetry
          handbook, running: a span leaving a service, picking up what the
          collector adds to it, and landing in a backend. Click a different one
          and you follow that one instead.
        </p>
        <p class="hero__cta">
          <a class="cta cta--primary" href={url("handbooks")}>Browse the handbooks</a>
          <a class="cta" href={url("docs")}>Read the documentation</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="hero__note mono">
        <p>Live simulation. Not a video.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="hero-stage">
      <HeroSim client:visible />
    </section>

    <section class="editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What a lab is</h2>
        <p>
          Every lab answers one concrete question, and it answers it at several
          depths. The outside view shows the parts and what moves between them.
          Go one level down and you are inside a part. Keep going and you reach
          the thing itself — the bytes on the wire, or the wire that is either
          high or low.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those are not separate drawings. They are views of one running state,
          so what you read at the bottom is what produced the picture at the top.
          Only the leaves have behaviour; everything above is the result of
          running what is inside it. When a lab makes a claim, the repository
          has a way to check it against the real thing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="sidenote">
        <p>
          Sourced from the OpenTelemetry specification and official docs. The
          teaching order follows <em>Learning OpenTelemetry</em>, because the
          order in which the concepts hold each other up is right there.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="levels">
      <p class="levels__intro">
        Underneath every handbook is one engine: a graph you can go into. It
        draws a running flow, and each layer of abstraction opens into the one
        below — not another diagram of the same thing, the same thing seen
        closer.
      </p>
      <ol class="levels__list">
        {
          GESTOS.map((gesto) => (
            <li class="levels__item">
              <span class="levels__name">{gesto.name}</span>
              <span class="levels__line">{gesto.line}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
      <p class="levels__note mono">
        And when a subject is not a flow with layers — or when the thing that
        matters is one item and not the pipeline it crosses — the lab is a
        different one, built for it. A street is a poor way to explain a door.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="path" id="handbooks">
      <div class="path__intro">
        <h2>The handbooks</h2>
        <p>
          Each one is a <code>.model</code> on the same engine, and each one has
          the same anatomy: a roadmap, articles, and labs. Open one to see where
          it stands — nothing here claims to be finished when it is not.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="handbooks">
        {HANDBOOKS.map((handbook) => <HandbookCard handbook={handbook} />)}
      </div>
      <DocsState />
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-block: var(--space-5) var(--space-3);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-3);
    max-width: 15ch;
  }

  .hero__lede {
    font-size: var(--size-step-1);
  }

  .hero__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .hero-stage,
  .path {
    max-width: 92rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--space-3);
  }

  .hero-stage {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-5);
  }

  .path {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .path__intro {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-3);
  }

  section.editorial {
    padding-block: var(--space-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    margin-block: 0 var(--space-2);
  }

  .sidenote {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    border-block-start: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-1);
    align-self: start;
  }

  .hero__cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
  }

  .cta {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.5rem var(--space-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-2);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .cta:hover {
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .cta--primary {
    background: var(--accent);
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  .levels {
    max-width: 92rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
  }

  .levels__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-3);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .levels__item {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 12rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    padding-block: var(--space-1);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .levels__name {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--size-step-1);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .levels__line {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  .handbooks {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(20rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .levels__intro {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .levels__note {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }
</style>
